{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-67262",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate $v,$ so we will treat the other variables $d$ and $t$ as constants. Since $v$ is being multiplied by $t,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =v\\cdot t \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =\\frac{v\\cdot t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =\\frac{v\\cdot \\cancel{t}}{\\cancel{t}} \\\\ \\frac{d}{t} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $v=\\frac{d}{t}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $t\\ne 0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
